course: religion-ethics
lesson: 32
title: Hinduism and Sikhism
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Hinduism is one of the oldest religions in the world; it began in India and has many traditions, and many Hindus see the many gods as forms of one supreme reality called Brahman.
explain: Hindus believe that what we do has consequences (karma) and that souls are born again and again until they reach freedom (moksha); many also follow ahimsa, non-violence to all living things.
explain: Sikhism was founded about 500 years ago in the Punjab by Guru Nanak, who taught that there is one God and that all people are equal, whatever their family, wealth or religion.
explain: In every gurdwara there is a langar, a free kitchen where anyone may sit on the floor and eat together, rich and poor side by side; this is seva, selfless service.
explain: For a Christian child this means your Hindu and Sikh neighbours have deep reasons for what they do, and Christians, too, are called to serve and share; you can admire their kindness and still keep your own faith.
fact: Hinduism began in India and is one of the oldest religions in the world.
fact: Many Hindus believe the many gods are forms of one supreme reality, Brahman.
fact: Hindus believe in karma: good and bad actions bring their own results.
fact: Hindus believe the soul is reborn many times (reincarnation) until it reaches freedom, called moksha.
fact: A Hindu temple is called a mandir, and worship there with flowers, light and food is called puja.
fact: Diwali is the festival of lights in autumn, celebrating good over evil; Hindus, Sikhs and Jains (followers of another old Indian religion) celebrate it.
fact: Ahimsa means non-violence to all living things; it is why many Hindus are vegetarian.
fact: Sikhism was founded by Guru Nanak in the Punjab about 500 years ago.
fact: Sikhs believe in one God and the equality of all people.
fact: The Sikh place of worship is the gurdwara, and its free kitchen is the langar.
fact: The Sikh holy book, the Guru Granth Sahib, is treated with honour as a living Guru.
fact: Initiated Sikhs, who have made special promises in a ceremony, wear the five Ks, including uncut hair (kesh), often under a turban, and a steel bracelet (kara).
fact: Seva means selfless service to others, and it is central in Sikhism.
exercise: Where did Hinduism begin? | India | Arabia | Israel | China
exercise: What do many Hindus call the one supreme reality behind the many gods? | Brahman | Moksha | Karma | Puja
exercise: What is karma? | The idea that actions bring their own results | A festival with lamps and sweets | The Hindu holy city on the banks of the great river Ganges | A Sikh prayer said at dawn
exercise: What do Hindus believe happens to the soul after death? | It is reborn until it reaches moksha | It sleeps for ever in the ground where the body lies | It turns into a star in the night sky above | It becomes a god straight away and never returns
exercise: What is a Hindu temple called? | A mandir | A gurdwara | A mosque | A synagogue
exercise: What is puja? | Worship with offerings of flowers, light and food | A free meal for everyone at the temple | The steel bracelet worn by Sikhs | A month of fasting from dawn to dusk
exercise: What does Diwali celebrate? | Good winning over evil, light over darkness | The birth of Guru Nanak in a stable in Bethlehem | The end of a month of fasting | The first harvest of the year
exercise: What is ahimsa? | Non-violence towards all living things | Giving a tenth of your money away to the temple | Praying five times every day facing the river | Wearing your hair under a turban at all times
exercise: Why are many Hindus vegetarian? | Because of ahimsa, respect for all life | Because meat is too expensive in India | Because the Vedas forbid all cooking of any kind | Because they fast every Sunday
exercise: Who founded Sikhism? | Guru Nanak | The Buddha | Muhammad | Brahman
exercise: What did Guru Nanak teach about people? | All people are equal before God | Only priests can speak to God for other people | Rich people are closer to God than poor people | Each family has its own god to look after it
exercise: What is the langar? | A free kitchen where everyone eats together | The Sikh holy book on a stand | A festival of lamps in autumn | A prayer mat used by worshippers in the gurdwara
exercise: What is the Sikh holy book? | The Guru Granth Sahib | The Qur'an | The Bible | The Bhagavad Gita
exercise: How do Sikhs treat their holy book? | With honour, as a living Guru | As an ordinary library book anyone can borrow | As a book only for scholars and priests to study | As a secret that no one may ever open or read
exercise: Which of these is one of the five Ks? | Kesh, uncut hair | Karma, good deeds | Kosher, allowed food | Kirtan, a hymn book
exercise: What is seva? | Selfless service to others | A Hindu god of light | A fast before a festival | A temple bell rung at dawn
exercise: Which faiths celebrate Diwali? | Hindus, Sikhs and Jains | Only Hindus in India | Christians and Muslims | Only Buddhists in Tibet
test: Harpreet's little brother asks why his dad wears a turban. What is the best answer? | Initiated Sikhs keep their hair uncut and often wear it in a turban | It keeps his head warm in the gurdwara | Every Indian man has to wear one | It shows he is a Hindu priest
test: my mate says Hindus worship like a million random gods so they're confused lol. fair? | Not fair; many Hindus see the gods as forms of one reality | Yes, each Hindu just chooses a brand new god every single day | Yes, Hindus do not believe in anything deeper | No, Hindus have no gods at all
test: You visit a gurdwara and a man offers you food in the langar. Do you have to be a Sikh? | No, everyone is welcome to eat, whatever their faith | Yes, only Sikhs may eat in the langar | Only if you pay at the door first | Only on the day of Diwali
test: Priya says: "If I'm mean today, it comes back to me." Which Hindu idea is she thinking of? | Karma | Moksha | Puja | Seva
test: Your sister says Diwali is a Christian holiday with fairy lights. Put her right. | It is a festival of lights for Hindus, Sikhs and Jains | It is a Muslim feast at the end of the fast | It is the Sikh day of the holy book's birth, kept in summer | It is a Christian feast of candles in spring
test: Arjun won't swat a fly and says it's ahimsa. What does he mean? | He tries not to harm any living thing | He thinks flies are lucky in his family | He is fasting from all hard work today | He is scared of insects since he was little
test: Why do people in the langar sit together on the floor? | To show that everyone is equal | Because the gurdwara has no money for chairs | Because the floor is warmer than chairs | So the priests can sit higher up
test: Which is true about Sikhism? | It teaches one God and the equality of all people | It teaches many gods, many castes and many holy books | It began in Arabia 1400 years ago | It was founded by the Buddha
test: crap, I put my juice on the shelf by their holy book at Jas's house. why's his mum upset? | Sikhs honour the Guru Granth Sahib as a living Guru | Juice is forbidden in every Sikh home | She wanted the juice for herself | The shelf is only for the TV remote
test: judge asks: "Is Christian service like Sikh seva?" Best answer? | Both serve others selflessly, from different beliefs | No, Christians are never asked to serve | Yes, they are exactly the same belief with different names | No, seva means praying, not helping
test: What do Hindus call the freedom when the soul stops being reborn? | Moksha | Karma | Ahimsa | Kara
test: Ravi's nan takes flowers and a lamp to the mandir. What is she doing? | Puja, worship with offerings | Langar, serving free food to all | Hajj, a holy journey to the city | Seva, cleaning the shoes of visitors
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the Hindu doctrine of reincarnation? | The soul is reborn repeatedly until liberation, termed moksha, is attained | The soul is extinguished entirely upon the death of the body | The soul ascends immediately and permanently unto heaven | The soul is reborn but once, in the form of an animal
exam: Identify the principal teaching of Guru Nanak concerning humankind. | That there is one God and all persons are equal | That there are many gods for many peoples | That priests alone may approach God | That wealth is a sign of divine favour and holiness
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the langar. | It is a kitchen in which a meal is provided gratis to all visitors | It is a chamber reserved for the holy book alone | It is a festival during which lamps are lit | It is a meal reserved exclusively for initiated Sikhs
exam: Ascertain the meaning of the term ahimsa as employed in Hinduism. | The principle of non-violence towards all living beings | The principle of selfless service rendered within the temple | The obligation to fast during the hours of daylight | The obligation to wear five articles of faith
